{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"85655d8d-d6a0-475d-914a-3224c130cda8","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A lever turns around a pivot. Torque measures how strongly a force tends to turn it.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Torque on a lever"},{"op":"diagram","id":"lever","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.15,0.6],[0.88,0.6]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"rigid horizontal lever"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.09,0.74],[0.15,0.6],[0.21,0.74],[0.09,0.74]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"fixed pivot supporting the lever"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.15,0.8],"content":"pivot"},{"kind":"point","at":[0.8,0.6],"meaning":"force application point"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"85655d8d-d6a0-475d-914a-3224c130cda8","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Push upward at the marked point. The perpendicular force turns the lever counterclockwise; its distance from the pivot matters.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"force","canvas_id":"lever","from":[0.8,0.6],"to":[0.8,0.23],"stroke":"solid","label":"F = 10 N"},{"op":"line","id":"distance","canvas_id":"lever","from":[0.15,0.5],"to":[0.8,0.5],"stroke":"dashed","label":"r = 0.40 m"},{"op":"equation","id":"torque_eq","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\tau = rF \\quad (F\\perp\\text{lever})"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"85655d8d-d6a0-475d-914a-3224c130cda8","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Multiply force by the perpendicular distance: ten newtons at forty centimeters produces four newton meters of counterclockwise torque.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"calculation","anchor":{"el":"torque_eq","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\tau=(0.40\\,\\mathrm{m})(10\\,\\mathrm{N})=4.0\\,\\mathrm{N\\,m}"},{"op":"text","id":"direction","anchor":{"el":"calculation","side":"below","gap":0.06},"content":"Counterclockwise"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"85655d8d-d6a0-475d-914a-3224c130cda8","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For an angled push, only the perpendicular component contributes. A force directed along the lever produces no torque.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"general","anchor":{"el":"direction","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\tau=rF\\sin\\theta"},{"op":"text","id":"angle_note","anchor":{"el":"general","side":"below","gap":0.05},"content":"Angle between lever arm and force"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"With the same perpendicular force applied twice as far from the pivot, what happens to torque?","expected_gist":"Torque doubles because torque equals force times perpendicular distance."}}}
